      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes that can be formed with 12 points in space (non-collinear), we need to find out the number of ways we can select 3 points out of the 12. This is a classic combination problem where we use the combination formula:\n\nC(n, k) = n! / (k!(n-k)!)\n\nWhere:\n- n is the total number of items (points), which is 12 in this case.\n- k is the number of items to be chosen (points to form a plane), which is 3 in this case.\n\nLet's calculate C(12, 3):\n\nC(12, 3) = 12! / (3!(12-3)!)\nC(12, 3) = 12! / (3!9!)\nC(12, 3) = (12 × 11 × 10 × 9!) / (3 × 2 × 1 × 9!)\nC(12, 3) = (12 × 11 × 10) / (3 × 2 × 1)\nC(12, 3) = 1320 / 6\nC(12, 3) = 220\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is 220.",
