      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the maximum number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space, given that no three points are collinear. Each plane is uniquely defined by three non-collinear points, and since we have 12 points and no three points are collinear, each pair of points can form a unique plane. This is equivalent to finding the number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of points, \\( k \\) is the number of points to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\nHere, \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\( 9! \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]",
