      "text": "To determine the maximum possible number of planes, we need to consider that each plane is uniquely defined by three non-collinear points in space. So, we need to find the maximum number of unique triples (sets of three points) that can be formed from twelve points.\n\nRecall the combination formula, which allows you to calculate the number of ways to choose $k$ items from a collection of $n$ items without regard to the order of selection. The formula is:\n\n\\[ \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\]\n\nIn this case, we want to choose 3 points out of 12, so $n = 12$ and $k = 3$. The formula becomes:\n\n\\[ \\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3!9!} \\]\n\nLet's calculate this value.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the combination of 12 choose 3\nn = 12\nk = 3\nmax_planes = math.comb(n, k)\nprint(max_planes)\n```\n```output\n220\n```\nThe maximum possible number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
